[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Two Pointers and Sliding Window Techniques"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Efficient array and string manipulation is a cornerstone of technical interviews. Two fundamental algorithmic paradigms used to optimize linear data structure problems are the Two Pointers technique and the Sliding Window technique. Both approaches typically reduce time complexity from O(n^2) to O(n)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Two Pointers Technique"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Two Pointers pattern uses two integer variables to traverse an array or string, usually moving towards each other or in the same direction at different speeds. It is most effective on sorted arrays or palindromes."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Opposite Direction: One pointer starts at the beginning (left = 0) and the other at the end (right = n - 1). Useful for problems like 'Two Sum II' or checking palindromes."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Same Direction: Both pointers start at the beginning, but move at different paces. Useful for removing duplicates or partitioning arrays."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"function isPalindrome(s) {
  let left = 0;
  let right = s.length - 1;
  while (left < right) {
    if (s[left] !== s[right]) return false;
    left++;
    right--;
  }
  return true;
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Sliding Window Technique"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Sliding Window pattern is a variation of the two pointers approach where pointers define a window (a subset of elements). Instead of recalculating the window properties from scratch when it moves, we slide the window by adding the new element entering on the right and removing the old element leaving on the left."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fixed Window Size: The window size 'k' remains constant. We slide it across the array to find maximum or minimum sums."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dynamic Window Size: The window size expands and shrinks based on a specific condition, such as finding the longest substring without repeating characters."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"function maxSubarraySum(arr, k) {
  let maxSum = 0;
  let windowSum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) {
    windowSum += arr[i];
  }
  maxSum = windowSum;
  for (let i = k; i < arr.length; i++) {
    windowSum = windowSum - arr[i - k] + arr[i];
    maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, windowSum);
  }
  return maxSum;
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Always look for sequential data problems involving subarrays, substrings, or sorted pairs. If a brute-force approach requires nested loops resulting in O(n^2) complexity, consider whether a Two Pointers or Sliding Window technique can solve the problem in a single pass."}]
